      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218944/assets/136b4d945288f7492273d66b1795e82183699b14fe898b4537e16fc36ad5bf5b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218944/assets/15b9b0530dbd4a193efb2ffd6d7719472c6aa641714298c4644ae47d167cf175.png)",
